  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Given a sequence XXX of length MMM, f(X)f(X)f(X) is defined as f(X)=∑i=1M−1∣Xi+1−Xi∣f(X) = \\sum_{i = 1}^{M - 1} |X_{i + 1} - X_i|f(X)=∑i=1M−1​∣Xi+1​−Xi​∣.\n\nSpecifically, if M=1M = 1M=1, then f(X)=0f(X) = 0f(X)=0.\n\nFor e.g., f([3,1,7,2])=∣1−3∣+∣7−1∣+∣2−7∣=13f([3, 1, 7, 2]) = |1-3| + |7-1| + |2-7| = 13f([3,1,7,2])=∣1−3∣+∣7−1∣+∣2−7∣=13\n\nJJ has an array AAA of length NNN. He wants to divide the array AAA into two subsequences PPP and QQQ (possibly empty) such that the value of f(P)+f(Q)f(P) + f(Q)f(P)+f(Q) is as large as possible. (Note that each AiA_iAi​ must belong to either subsequence PPP or subsequence QQQ).\n\nHelp him find this maximum value of f(P)+f(Q)f(P) + f(Q)f(P)+f(Q).\n\nNote: A sequence XXX is a subsequence of a sequence YYY if XXX can be obtained from YYY by deletion of several (possibly, zero or all) elements.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains TTT - the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN - the size of the array AAA.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\dots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ denoting the array AAA.\n\n\nOutput Format\nOutput the largest value of f(P)+f(Q)f(P) + f(Q)f(P)+f(Q).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤2001 \\leq T \\leq 2001≤T≤200",
      "1≤N≤10001 \\leq N \\leq 10001≤N≤1000",
      "1≤Ai≤1051 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^51≤Ai​≤105",
      "It is guaranteed that the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 300030003000."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n6\n1 4 6 3 4 2\n5\n1 2 3 4 5\n2\n1 10",
        "output": "11\n6\n9",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case-1: One way to divide the array into two subsequences PPP and QQQ as follows:\nP=[1,6,2]P = [1, 6, 2]P=[1,6,2], Q=[4,3,4]Q = [4, 3, 4]Q=[4,3,4].\nf(P)=∣6−1∣+∣2−6∣=9f(P) = |6 - 1| + |2 - 6| = 9f(P)=∣6−1∣+∣2−6∣=9 and f(Q)=∣3−4∣+∣4−3∣=2f(Q) = |3 - 4| + |4 - 3| = 2f(Q)=∣3−4∣+∣4−3∣=2.\nTherefore f(P)+f(Q)=11f(P) + f(Q) = 11f(P)+f(Q)=11.\nIt can be proven that this is the maximum value of f(P)+f(Q)f(P) + f(Q)f(P)+f(Q).\nTest case-2: One way to divide the array into two subsequences PPP and QQQ as follows:\nP=[1,4]P = [1, 4]P=[1,4], Q=[2,3,5]Q = [2, 3, 5]Q=[2,3,5].\nf(P)=∣4−1∣=3f(P) = |4 - 1|= 3f(P)=∣4−1∣=3 and f(Q)=∣3−2∣+∣5−3∣=3f(Q) = |3 - 2| + |5 - 3| = 3f(Q)=∣3−2∣+∣5−3∣=3.\nTherefore f(P)+f(Q)=6f(P) + f(Q) = 6f(P)+f(Q)=6.\nIt can be proven that this is the maximum value of f(P)+f(Q)f(P) + f(Q)f(P)+f(Q).\nTest case-3: One way to divide the array into two subsequences PPP and QQQ as follows:\nP=[1,10]P = [1, 10]P=[1,10], Q=[]Q = []Q=[].\nf(P)=∣10−1∣=9f(P) = |10 - 1| = 9f(P)=∣10−1∣=9 and f(Q)=0f(Q) = 0f(Q)=0.\nTherefore f(P)+f(Q)=9f(P) + f(Q) = 9f(P)+f(Q)=9.\nIt can be proven that this is the maximum value of f(P)+f(Q)f(P) + f(Q)f(P)+f(Q)."
      }
    ]
